{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8f8511b442d753f7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\n2\t\n3\tPitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis\n4\t\n5\t Title:\n6\t\n7\t Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\n8\t\n9\t Description:\n10\t\n11\t arXiv is now an independent nonprofit!\n12\t Learn more\n13\t ×\n14\t\n15\t License: CC BY 4.0\n16\t\n17\tarXiv:2608.10979v1 [cs.SD] 11 Aug 2026\n18\t\n19\tPitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis\n20\t\n21\tAbstract\n22\t\n23\tComputational analysis of music often relies on discrete representations, yet many musical traditions are organized around continuous pitch movement that resists segmentation into note-like units. For such traditions, the discrete units that analysis would build on are not given in advance. We address this gap by learning a vocabulary of local pitch-contour patterns directly from unlabeled audio, using a VQ-VAE that quantizes fixed-length contour segments into a finite codebook. To make the learned tokens stable across segmentation positions and small variations in timing and pitch range, we train the model with a reconstruction objective evaluated under the best alignment among a set of candidate temporal and pitch-domain transformations. Applied to Korean traditional music, the learned tokens recover information about expert-defined sigimsae categories without supervision, and in pansori individual tokens align with the two principal modes, Gyemyeonjo and Ujo, supporting their use as units for corpus-level analysis of contour-centric traditions.\n24\t\n25\t1 Introduction\n26\t\n27\tFigure 1: Overview of the proposed model.\n28\t\n29\tRepresenting music as sequences of discrete units has been one of the central abstractions underlying computational musicology and symbolic music information retrieval. Its analytical value lies in the fact that transitions between musical events can be counted and modeled statistically. For example, corpus-based chord progression analysis [12] can construct transition matrices between chord symbols and examine their distributional properties, which is possible because harmonic events are represented as discrete categories. A similar principle underlies probabilistic models of melody: once a melody is represented as a sequence of symbolic events, the probability or information content of each note can be estimated from its musical context. Temperley’s theory of uniform information density in music builds on this idea, explaining musical organization in terms of the probability and information density of musical events [21]. The same logic also extends to performance data: MIDI represents performances as note events with onset time, duration, pitch, and velocity, allowing timing and dynamics to be analyzed as event-level attributes [24]. In this sense, symbolic representations are not merely encodings of music, but interfaces that make musical structure available to computational analysis.\n30\t\n31\tThe utility of discrete representations, however, is fundamentally contingent on the availability of appropriate discrete units. Automatic music transcription (AMT) has made substantial progress in converting audio into note-level symbolic representations, yet existing systems are developed and evaluated primarily on Western music [2]. This limitation is not merely a matter of musical style or timbral diversity; it reflects an assumption that musically meaningful events can be segmented into note-like units with relatively well-defined pitch and duration. Many musical traditions do not fully conform to this premise. In such traditions, expressive content is often realized as continuous pitch movement rather than as a succession of clearly bounded note events, making conventional transcription pipelines ill-suited as a basis for analysis.\n32\t\n33\tOne way to obtain discrete units in such traditions is to rely on human expertise.\n34\tSome studies use expert-labeled events directly: for instance, a recent study on Carnatic music used expert-labeled svara and svara-form annotations to show that the svara-form performed for a given svara is strongly influenced by neighboring svaras [16].\n35\tOthers define a vocabulary of intermediate units in advance and transcribe recordings into it: the IDTAP platform, for example, represents Hindustani melodic practice using manually specified pitch-contour trajectories as a middle-level representation between raw F0 curves and conventional notation [13].\n36\tBoth directions show that discrete representations of contour-centric music can support analysis, but they share a reliance on predefined categories and manual labor, which is costly to scale and ill-suited to traditions where the relevant ornamental vocabulary is not formally codified.\n37\tThis motivates a different question: can we learn a discrete vocabulary of contour units directly from data?\n38\t\n39\tIn this work, we investigate this question through VQ-VAE-based pitch contour tokenization.\n40\tVQ-VAE provides a natural framework for this purpose because it learns a finite set of latent codes while preserving enough information to reconstruct the input contour. We use this mechanism not as a generative objective in itself, but as a way to induce a compact vocabulary of local contour patterns that can be used for musical analysis.\n41\tWe apply this approach to sigimsae, the ornamental system of Korean traditional music, where expressive musical units are often realized as continuous pitch movements rather than conventional note events.\n42\t\n43\tOur contribution is threefold: (i) a VQ-VAE-based pitch-contour tokenization model that learns a discrete vocabulary of local contour patterns without labels; (ii) a transformation-minimized reconstruction loss that makes token learning robust to small temporal and pitch-domain variations; and (iii) an evaluation via segmentation-shift consistency, sigimsae classification, and qualitative mode analysis in pansori. Our code and a demo page with audio examples are publicly available.11\n44\t 1\n45\t\n46\t Codebase: https://github.com/SeongUkJu/pitch-contour-tokenizer, Demo: https://seongukju.github.io/pitch-contour-tokenizer-page/\n47\t\n48\t2 Related Work\n49\t\n50\t2.1 Pitch-Contour Abstractions for Ornamental Music\n51\t\n52\tA notable precedent is found in Indian art music, where quantized pitch contours have served as an intermediate representation capturing ornamental detail beyond note-level transcription.\n53\tRanjani et al. [17, 18] mapped each pitch value onto a music-theoretically defined grid of note positions and showed that quantizing only at contour critical points preserves rāga and gamaka characteristics.\n54\tShikarpur et al. [20] adopted finely quantized pitch contours (10-cent bins) as an intermediate representation for hierarchical generative modeling of Hindustani vocal music.\n55\tBoth discretize pitch values onto a predefined grid; we instead learn a vocabulary over local pitch patterns (fixed-length contour segments) directly from unlabeled data.\n56\t\n57\tA separate line of work mines recurring melodic patterns from raw contours via similarity search.\n58\tGulati et al. [6] used DTW-based similarity over a 365-hour Carnatic corpus, and Nuttall et al. [14] applied the matrix profile to find repeated motifs within a single recording.\n59\tThese methods identify a small set of variable-length, recurrence-based instances that highlight reuse, but they do not yield a reusable vocabulary that covers every frame.\n60\tOur tokenizer, by contrast, aims to produce a dense token sequence over the entire contour using a fixed codebook, which makes the resulting representation directly amenable to corpus-level statistical analysis.\n61\t\n62\t2.2 VQ-VAE and Discrete Representation Learning\n63\t\n64\tVQ-VAE [23] learns discrete latent codes through\n65\treconstruction-based training. Given an input 𝐱\\mathbf{x}, an encoder\n66\tfθf_{\\theta} produces a continuous embedding 𝐳e=fθ​(𝐱)\\mathbf{z}_{e}=f_{\\theta}(\\mathbf{x}),\n67\twhich is replaced by its nearest entry in a learned codebook\n68\tℰ={𝐞k}k=1K\\mathcal{E}=\\{\\mathbf{e}_{k}\\}_{k=1}^{K} via\n69\t𝐳q=𝐞k∗\\mathbf{z}_{q}=\\mathbf{e}_{k^{\\ast}}, k∗=arg⁡mink⁡‖𝐳e−𝐞k‖2k^{\\ast}=\\arg\\min_{k}\\|\\mathbf{z}_{e}-\\mathbf{e}_{k}\\|_{2},\n70\tand a decoder gϕg_{\\phi} reconstructs 𝐱^=gϕ​(𝐳q)\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}=g_{\\phi}(\\mathbf{z}_{q}).\n71\tThe model is trained with\n72\t\n73\tℒ=ℒrec+‖sg⁡[𝐳e]−𝐳q‖22+β​‖𝐳e−sg⁡[𝐳q]‖22,\\mathcal{L}=\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{rec}}+\\|\\mathrm{sg}[\\mathbf{z}_{e}]-\\mathbf{z}_{q}\\|_{2}^{2}+\\beta\\,\\|\\mathbf{z}_{e}-\\mathrm{sg}[\\mathbf{z}_{q}]\\|_{2}^{2},\n74\t\n75\t(1)\n76\t\n77\twhere sg⁡[⋅]\\mathrm{sg}[\\cdot] is the stop-gradient operator and gradients flow\n78\tthrough the quantization step via the straight-through estimator.\n79\t\n80\tIn music and audio, vector quantization has been used primarily for generation and compression of audio waveforms or symbolic sequences [7, 5, 25, 4], where codes are optimized for fidelity or bitrate rather than interpretability.\n81\t\n82\tOur use of VQ-VAE differs from both lines: the input is a one-dimensional\n83\tpitch contour 𝐱∈ℝL\\mathbf{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L}, and the learned codes are\n84\tintended as interpretable analytical units rather than for generation or\n85\tcompression.\n86\tIn speech, vector-quantized autoencoders applied to raw or spectral audio have been shown to discover units that align with phonetic content [3], supporting the use of VQ-VAE for unsupervised unit discovery from continuous signals.\n87\t\n88\t3 VQ-VAE on Pitch Contour\n89\t\n90\tThe overall architecture is based on VQ-VAE, using a stack of 1D convolutional layers, with two key design choices to enable interpretable, segmentation-robust (position-invariant) tokenization of pitch contours, as illustrated in Figure 1.\n91\t\n92\t3.1 Receptive Field Separation\n93\t\n94\tTo ensure a 1:1 correspondence between each discrete token and a fixed-length pitch contour segment, the input is pre-segmented into non-overlapping windows of a fixed size before encoding.\n95\tThis prevents inter-token dependency during decoding and promotes interpretability of the learned codebook by constraining each token to represent a single localized contour segment.\n96\t\n97\tTo direct the codebook toward capturing ornamental contour patterns rather than absolute pitch levels, we subtract the median pitch value of each receptive field segment before encoding.\n98\tThis makes the objective insensitive to absolute pitch offset and encourages each entry to represent local movements such as oscillations and slides.\n99\tWhen needed for visualization of the full contour sequence, the decoded contour can be shifted back by adding the subtracted median.\n100\t\n101\t3.2 Transformation-Minimized Reconstruction Loss\n102\t\n103\tBecause the input contour is divided into fixed-size non-overlapping segments before encoding, the assigned token can be sensitive to the arbitrary segmentation position.\n104\tFor example, if a vibrato pattern is shifted by only a few frames, the phase observed within a segment changes, although the underlying oscillatory pattern remains the same.\n105\tThis matters because the model is trained with a frame-level MSE reconstruction loss:\n106\teven when two contours correspond to the same local ornamental pattern, small temporal misalignments can lead to large reconstruction errors.\n107\tWithout an alignment-aware objective, the model may encode incidental frame-level differences as separate entries rather than a stable representation of the underlying pattern.\n108\t\n109\tWe address this issue by minimizing the reconstruction error over four transformation factors: (i) temporal offset, which accounts for arbitrary segmentation position;\n110\t(ii) temporal scale, which accounts for small duration differences;\n111\t(iii) pitch offset, which accounts for residual vertical mismatch between the target and reconstruction; and\n112\t(iv) pitch-range scale, which accounts for amplitude or range differences in otherwise similar contour shapes.\n113\tWe define a set of candidate transformations consisting of temporal alignment, pitch shifting, and pitch-range scaling.\n114\t\n115\tLet 𝐱∈ℝL\\mathbf{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L} be a median-normalized input segment and 𝐱^∈ℝL′\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L^{\\prime}} be the decoder output, where L′>LL^{\\prime}>L.\n116\t\n117\tWe define a transformed reconstruction candidate as\n118\t\n119\tTs,τ,a,b​(𝐱^)=a⋅Cτ​(Rs​(𝐱^))+b,T_{s,\\tau,a,b}(\\hat{\\mathbf{x}})=a\\cdot C_{\\tau}\\left(R_{s}(\\hat{\\mathbf{x}})\\right)+b,\n120\t\n121\t(2)\n122\t\n123\twhere Rs​(⋅)R_{s}(\\cdot) resamples the decoded contour with temporal scale ss, Cτ​(⋅)C_{\\tau}(\\cdot) extracts a length-LL crop at temporal offset τ\\tau, aa scales the pitch range, and bb shifts the pitch level.\n124\tA length-LL crop is then extracted with temporal offset τ∈𝒯\\tau\\in\\mathcal{T}:\n125\t\n126\t𝐱^(s,τ)=Cτ​(𝐱^(s)),\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}^{(s,\\tau)}=C_{\\tau}\\left(\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}^{(s)}\\right),\n127\t\n128\t(3)\n129\t\n130\twhere Cτ​(⋅)C_{\\tau}(\\cdot) returns a contiguous segment of length LL starting at offset τ\\tau.\n131\t\n132\tThe reconstruction loss is computed as the minimum MSE over all candidate transformations:\n133\t\n134\tℒrec=mins∈𝒮,τ∈𝒯,a∈𝒜,b∈ℬ⁡1L​‖𝐱−[a⋅Cτ​(Rs​(𝐱^))+b]‖22.\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{rec}}=\\min_{s\\in\\mathcal{S},\\ \\tau\\in\\mathcal{T},\\ a\\in\\mathcal{A},\\ b\\in\\mathcal{B}}\\frac{1}{L}\\left\\|\\mathbf{x}-\\left[a\\cdot C_{\\tau}\\left(R_{s}(\\hat{\\mathbf{x}})\\right)+b\\right]\\right\\|_{2}^{2}.\n135\t\n136\t(4)\n137\t\n138\tOnly the transformation attaining the minimum error is used for backpropagation.\n139\t\n140\tThis can be interpreted as fitting the model under the best alignment between the decoded contour and the target segment, rather than penalizing all frame-level discrepancies directly. This objective encourages the codebook to represent contour patterns that are stable under small temporal and pitch-domain variations.\n141\t\n142\tFor example, vibrato contours with similar periodic structure can be mapped to the same token even when they differ in phase or amplitude, because the loss evaluates reconstruction after the best temporal and pitch-domain alignment.\n143\t\n144\t4 Experimental Setup\n145\t\n146\t4.1 Dataset\n147\t\n148\tIn this study, we apply VQ-VAE to pitch contours of Korean traditional music. Although the proposed method is applicable to any pitch contour-based musical tradition, such as Indian art music or pop vocal singing, we focus on Korean traditional music.\n149\tThis is because continuous pitch movement is not merely an expressive embellishment but a central, defining element of the tradition. Unlike Western staff notation, in which music is conceived as a sequence of discrete note events, Korean traditional music, particularly its ornamental system sigimsae, is realized through fluid, continuous pitch trajectories in which the boundary between one “note” and the next is often ambiguous or undefined. This makes it an especially well-suited testbed for a model designed to discover meaningful structure from pitch contours without presupposing discrete note events.\n150\t\n151\tWe selected an in-house pansori recording dataset consisting of approximately 280 hours of audio recordings.\n152\tPansori is a representative vocal genre of Korean traditional music, sung by a solo singer with barrel drum accompaniment.\n153\tIt is well known for its extensive use of melodic ornamentation, making it a particularly rich source of the ornamental patterns investigated in this study.\n154\tFor pansori recordings, the vocal tracks are first isolated using HT-Demucs  [19] to remove instrumental accompaniment.\n155\t\n156\t4.1.1 Pre-processing\n157\t\n158\tWe extract the fundamental frequency (F0) with the pretrained CREPE [9], which estimates F0 values every 10 ms with a confidence score in [0,1][0,1].\n159\tThe extracted F0 values are converted to MIDI scale and normalized by median.\n160\tFrames below a predefined confidence threshold are filtered out.\n161\tWithin each receptive field segment, the gaps of up to two consecutive filtered frames are filled via linear interpolation.\n162\t\n163\tSegments containing extensive unvoiced regions are discarded because our 1D CNN-based tokenizer assumes a dense continuous contour and cannot directly represent missing pitch values.\n164\tFilling such regions with a constant padding value would introduce artificial patterns into the input, which could be absorbed by the codebook and hinder the interpretability of the learned representations in subsequent analysis.\n165\tAddressing unvoiced regions more explicitly is left for future work.\n166\t\n167\tBefore filtering, the data are split 8:1:1 into train, validation, and test sets.\n168\tDuring training, a random start point is sampled from each filtered segment at every epoch, introducing variability in segmentation position, while the validation and test sets use fixed segments for consistent evaluation.\n169\t\n170\t4.2 Model Specification\n171\t\n172\tThe encoder and decoder each consist of 6 stacked one-dimensional convolutional layers with a receptive field size of 128 frames and a codebook of 256 entries.\n173\tThe model is trained using the VQ-VAE objective [23], in which the standard reconstruction loss is replaced by the transformation-minimized reconstruction loss described in Section 3.2, combined with a vector quantization loss with commitment coefficient β=2.0\\beta=2.0.\n174\tTraining is conducted for 50K updates using the AdamW optimizer with an initial learning rate of 0.001 and a batch size of 1024.\n175\tWe select the checkpoint with the lowest validation reconstruction loss.\n176\t\n177\tThe transformation parameters are set empirically.\n178\tThe decoder produces 2​L2L frames, resampled by Rs​(⋅)R_{s}(\\cdot) to lengths LsL_{s} approximately 𝒮={1, 1.08, 1.2}\\mathcal{S}=\\{1,\\,1.08,\\,1.2\\} times LL (i.e., 128128, 139139, and 154154 frames); all valid offsets 𝒯={0,1,…,Ls−L}\\mathcal{T}=\\{0,1,\\dots,L_{s}-L\\} are considered as crops, where LsL_{s} is the resampled length.\n179\tPitch-range scaling and pitch shifting use 𝒜={0.85,1.0,1.15}\\mathcal{A}=\\{0.85,1.0,1.15\\} and ℬ={−0.1,0.0,0.1}\\mathcal{B}=\\{-0.1,0.0,0.1\\}.\n180\t\n181\tFor the comparison, we also trained an Autoencoder Baseline, adapting Nuttall et al. [15], who applied Uniform Manifold Approximation and Projection (UMAP) [11] and K-Means clustering to supervised GRU embeddings.\n182\tOur unsupervised variant trains an autoencoder on the pansori dataset and tokenizes its latent embeddings via z-score normalization, UMAP, and K-Means.\n183\t\n184\t5 Segmentation Shift Consistency\n185\t\n186\tMethod\n187\tKLD ↓\\downarrow\n188\tAcc. ↑\\uparrow\n189\t\n190\t0–60\n191\t0–124\n192\t0–60\n193\t0–124\n194\t\n195\tAutoencoder\n196\t1.747\n197\t2.085\n198\t0.100\n199\t0.115\n200\t\n201\tVQ-VAE\n202\t0.657\n203\t0.859\n204\t0.462\n205\t0.488\n206\t\n207\t  + temporal align\n208\t0.531\n209\t0.735\n210\t0.519\n211\t0.561\n212\t\n213\t  + all transformations\n214\t0.652\n215\t0.881\n216\t0.456\n217\t0.495\n218\t\n219\tTable 1: Token consistency evaluation results. KLD denotes Token Distribution KLD, and Acc. denotes Token Matching Accuracy. Results are reported for two segmentation-offset ranges: 0–60 and 0–124 frames. “+ temporal align” applies temporal offset and temporal-scale transformations, while “+ all transformations” additionally includes pitch offset and pitch-range scaling.\n220\t\n221\tWe introduced the transformation-minimized reconstruction loss to reduce the sensitivity of token assignment to temporal and pitch-domain variations.\n222\tHere, we isolate one of its intended effects: robustness to segmentation position.\n223\tSpecifically, we test whether the same long pitch contour yields similar token sequences when the starting position of the fixed-size segmentation window is shifted.\n224\t\n225\t5.1 Experimental Dataset Construction\n226\t\n227\tFrom the pansori dataset used for VQ-VAE training, we select contour segments that satisfy the filtering criteria, contain no unvoiced frames, and are at least three times longer than the receptive field. For each segment, we generate multiple token sequences by shifting the segmentation start position in increments of 4 frames.\n228\t\n229\tWe report results for two offset ranges: 0–60 frames and 0–124 frames. The former evaluates shifts smaller than half of the 128-frame receptive field, while the latter covers nearly all possible phases within one receptive field and thus provides a stricter test of segmentation robustness.\n230\t\n231\t5.2 Evaluation Metrics\n232\t\n233\tWe use two complementary metrics. Token Distribution KLD measures whether the overall codebook usage remains stable across shifted versions of the same contour. For each segment, we construct a reference token distribution by aggregating token counts across all shifted versions. We then compute the Kullback–Leibler divergence (KLD) between the token distribution of each shifted version and this reference distribution, and average the result over versions and segments. Lower KLD indicates more stable token usage.\n234\t\n235\tToken Matching Accuracy measures local token-level consistency. We use the offset-0 token sequence as the reference and compare each shifted token sequence against it. For offsets below half of the receptive field, tokens are compared at the same sequence index. For offsets of 64 frames or greater, each shifted token is compared with the succeeding reference token, since the shifted receptive field overlaps more strongly with the next reference window. Higher accuracy indicates more consistent token assignment across shifts.\n236\t\n237\t5.3 Results\n238\t\n239\tAs shown in Table 1, the autoencoder baseline is highly sensitive to segmentation shifts, whereas VQ-VAE substantially improves both distribution-level and local token consistency.\n240\t\n241\tAdding temporal alignment gives the best results on this segmentation-specific evaluation, supporting the intended effect of the transformation-minimized reconstruction loss. The full transformation setting performs worse than temporal alignment alone on this metric.\n242\tThis is expected because the additional pitch-domain transformations are not designed specifically for segmentation robustness; rather, they allow similar contour shapes to be matched after pitch shifting or pitch-range scaling.\n243\tAs a result, similar contours may be represented by different tokens whose decoded outputs can still be adjusted to fit the same target, which can reduce exact token consistency under shifted segmentations.\n244\tTheir benefit is therefore evaluated more directly in the downstream sigimsae classification experiment.\n245\t\n246\t6 Sigimsae Classification\n247\t\n248\tIf the learned tokens are to serve as analytical units, their patterns should align with expert-defined ornament categories. We test this with a minimal probing setup, a per-token MAP classifier — that isolates how much label-discriminative information is carried by individual tokens.\n249\t\n250\t6.1 Sigimsae Labeled Dataset\n251\t\n252\tWe employed the annotated Korean traditional music recordings from the NIA AI-Hub dataset [22].\n253\tThe dataset consists of annotated sigimsae segments from monophonic Korean traditional music recordings.\n254\tBecause it is highly class-imbalanced and contains both vocal and instrumental performances, we report results in two settings: the full setting, with 34.0 hours of audio and 42.3k annotated segments totaling 9.3 hours across six classes, and the vocal-only setting, with 10.0 hours of audio and 13.5k annotated segments totaling 2.4 hours across five classes.\n255\t\n256\tThe data are split at the track level with per-class stratification into train, validation, and test sets (approximately 8:1:1), ensuring that segments from the same track do not appear in multiple splits.\n257\t\n258\t6.2 Token-to-label lookup classifier\n259\t\n260\tFor each annotated sigimsae segment, the center portion is cropped to the receptive field size and encoded into a single token.\n261\tSegments shorter than the receptive field are padded with surrounding context.\n262\t\n263\tWe use a simple frequency-based classifier to test how much label-discriminative information is carried by individual tokens. This is not intended to build a strong supervised classifier. Rather, it serves as a probing task: if the tokens learned without sigimsae labels are musically meaningful, their distribution should contain information that is predictive of expert-defined ornament categories.\n264\t\n265\tFrom the training set, we count how often each token occurs with each sigimsae label and use these counts to estimate P⁡(token∣label)P(\\text{token}\\mid\\text{label}).\n266\tClassification is then performed using maximum a posteriori (MAP) estimation:\n267\t\n268\ty^=arg⁡max⁡[τ⋅log⁡P⁡(y)+log⁡P⁡(token∣y)]\\hat{y}=\\arg\\max\\left[\\tau\\cdot\\log P(y)+\\log P(\\text{token}\\mid y)\\right]\n269\t\n270\t(5)\n271\t\n272\twhere the temperature τ∈[0,1]\\tau\\in[0,1] is selected on the validation set and applied to the test set.\n273\t\n274\tAs a scale reference, we additionally report a supervised Encoder-Decoder Temporal Convolutional Network (ED-TCN), which was employed for Rāga ornamentation detection [10] trained directly on sigimsae classification with median-normalized F0 contours.\n275\tIt uses full label supervision and is not directly comparable, but indicates the order of magnitude reachable with supervised contour-based classification.\n276\t\n277\t6.3 Results\n278\t\n279\tMethod\n280\tF​1m​a​c​r​oF1_{macro} ↑\\uparrow\n281\tm​A​PmAP ↑\\uparrow\n282\t\n283\tAll\n284\tVocal\n285\tAll\n286\tVocal\n287\t\n288\tAutoencoder\n289\t0.284\n290\t0.324\n291\t0.265\n292\t0.290\n293\t\n294\tVQ-VAE\n295\t0.266\n296\t0.319\n297\t0.248\n298\t0.302\n299\t\n300\t  + temporal align\n301\t0.285\n302\t0.320\n303\t0.272\n304\t0.317\n305\t\n306\t  + all transformations\n307\t0.285\n308\t0.331\n309\t0.278\n310\t0.324\n311\t\n312\tED-TCN [10]\n313\t0.463\n314\t0.541\n315\t0.579\n316\t0.531\n317\t\n318\tTable 2: Sigimsae Classification results on NIA AI-Hub dataset [22]. We report F​1m​a​c​r​oF1_{macro} and m​A​PmAP for both all/vocal subset settings.\n319\t\n320\tAs presented in Table 2, vanilla VQ-VAE alone does not outperform the autoencoder baseline in F​1F1. Without alignment-aware training, similar contours are split across distinct codes, fragmenting per-class distributions. Adding the full transformation-minimized loss reverses this and yields the best probing scores across both settings, with a larger gain than temporal alignment alone.\n321\tThe gap to the supervised ED-TCN remains substantial, but a single token recovers roughly three-fifths of its F​1F1 on the vocal setting without sequence modeling or label supervision during representation learning.\n322\t\n323\talt.\n324\tFigure 2: Decoded contours of the most representative token per sigimsae category, selected by the highest P⁡(token∣label)P(\\textit{token}\\mid\\textit{label}), for VQ-VAE (left) and Autoencoder baseline (right).\n325\t\n326\tFigure 2 visualizes the per-class prototype token (highest OPENP⁡(token∣label))P(\\text{token}\\mid\\text{label})) for each model. For VQ-VAE, the six classes recruit six distinct tokens, each visually matching the canonical realization of its category: the upward-leap-and-glide of southern-style descending flick (Token 58), the monotonic descent of descending slide (Token 178), and three vibrato classes mapped to tokens with clearly distinct oscillation profiles.\n327\tFor the autoencoder, a single token (Token 196) is selected as the prototype for four of the six classes.\n328\tThe autoencoder’s embeddings entangle phase, pitch level, range, and shape, so K-Means cuts across ornament types.\n329\tThe transformation-minimized loss removes these factors during training, leaving contour shape as the main basis for the codebook.\n330\t\n331\tFigure 3: Decoded contours(black) of representative tokens for Gyemyeonjo (top) and Ujo, selected by the highest P⁡(mode∣token)P(\\text{mode}\\mid\\text{token}). Blue and red lines show the median-subtracted original contours assigned to each token for Gyemyeonjo and Ujo, respectively.\n332\t\n333\t7 Token Analysis in Pansori\n334\t\n335\tAs a case study, we explore whether the learned tokenizer can serve as a tool for data-driven musicological analysis by examining how individual tokens relate to the two principal modes of pansori.\n336\t\n337\tDataset:\n338\tPansori has two representative modes: Gyemyeonjo and Ujo, each characterized by distinct melodic and ornamental patterns.\n339\tWe used a mode-annotated subset [1] of our in-house pansori dataset, which was annotated by an expert. The dataset consists of 26.7 hours of Gyemyeonjo annotations and 8.7 hours of Ujo annotations.\n340\t\n341\tMethod: A pansori mode is largely defined by its scale, but each mode is also known to carry characteristic ornamental patterns. Because our tokens are median-normalized and thus carry no absolute pitch information, any mode-specific token usage must arise from contour shape itself. We therefore count token occurrences within mode-labeled segments and identify the tokens with the highest P⁡(mode∣token)P(\\text{mode}\\mid\\text{token}) for each mode, treating these as candidate ornamental patterns characteristic of each mode.\n342\t\n343\tResult: Figure 3 shows the contour of those representative tokens together with examples of input contours assigned to each token. To provide musical context, one of the co-authors also transcribed a slightly extended window around each example into staff notation.\n344\t\n345\tInspecting these tokens confirms that each is indeed characteristic of its associated mode. Token 87 captures the upper turn gesture, and Token 141 captures a downward gliding contour; both are widely described in pansori studies as defining ornamental features of Gyemyeonjo (Score Excerpts 24 and 3 in  [8]).\n346\tTokens 171, in contrast, corresponds to melodic phrase frequently observed in ujo.\n347\t\n348\tToken 54 is particularly informative. The descending major-second motion to the tonic that it captures is a melodic move shared by both Gyemyeonjo and Ujo, yet this specific token occurs predominantly in Ujo. The distinguishing factor is the wide vibrato applied to the upper note in Ujo before it resolves to the tonic. The tokenizer, trained without any mode supervision, separates the two modes along this ornamental dimension, a distinction that note-level transcription would discard since the underlying pitch sequence is identical.\n349\t\n350\tAlthough limited to a single case, this result indicates that the learned tokens can serve as analytical units linking raw contours to higher-level musicological categories. The same codebook, queried against other label sets such as schools or performers, offers a path toward corpus-level study of ornamental practice without requiring predefined ornament categories or note-level transcription.\n351\t\n352\t8 Conclusion and Future Work\n353\t\n354\tWe proposed a VQ-VAE-based tokenizer that learns a discrete vocabulary of local pitch-contour patterns without ornament labels, together with a transformation-minimized reconstruction loss that makes the learned tokens robust to small temporal and pitch-domain variations.\n355\tExperiments on Korean traditional music show improved segmentation-shift consistency and sigimsae classification over baselines, and qualitative analysis on pansori shows that individual tokens separate Gyemyeonjo and Ujo even when the underlying pitch sequence is shared, a distinction that note-level transcription would discard.\n356\t\n357\tOur approach has one main limitation: our 1D-CNN tokenizer assumes a dense continuous contour and cannot directly model unvoiced regions, so we restricted training and evaluation to segments with few unvoiced frames. Extending the model to handle unvoiced regions explicitly is necessary for tokenizing entire recordings end-to-end.\n358\t\n359\tTwo directions remain open for future work. The most immediate is corpus-level musicological analysis: converting long recordings into token sequences and studying transition statistics, n-gram distributions, or conditional patterns of ornamental units across modes, schools, and performers — analyses that have been routine for symbolic Western music but are not yet available for contour-centric traditions. The same framework is also in principle applicable to other such traditions, including Indian art music and pop vocal singing.\n360\t\n361\t9 Acknowledgement\n362\t\n363\tThis work was supported by the National Research Foundation of Korea (NRF) grant funded by the Korea government (MSIT) (RS-2025-00560548).\n364\t\n365\tReferences\n366\t\n367\t[1]\n368\tAnonymous (2026)\n369\t\n370\tFrame-level pansori mode classification with complementary audio representations.\n371\t\n372\tNote: Submitted to ISMIR 2026\n373\t\n374\tCited by: §7.\n375\t\n376\t[2]\n377\tS. Chang, E. Benetos, H. Kirchhoff, and S. Dixon (2024)\n378\t\n379\tYourmt3+: multi-instrument music transcription with enhanced transformer architectures and cross-dataset stem augmentation.\n380\t\n381\tIn 2024 IEEE 34th International Workshop on Machine Learning for Signal Processing (MLSP),\n382\t\n383\tpp. 1–6.\n384\t\n385\tCited by: §1.\n386\t\n387\t[3]\n388\tJ. Chorowski, R. J. Weiss, S. Bengio, and A. van den Oord (2019)\n389\t\n390\tUnsupervised speech representation learning using wavenet autoencoders.\n391\t\n392\tIEEE ACM Trans. Audio Speech Lang. Process. 27 (12), pp. 2041–2053.\n393\t\n394\tCited by: §2.2.\n395\t\n396\t[4]\n397\tA. Défossez, J. Copet, G. Synnaeve, and Y. Adi (2023)\n398\t\n399\tHigh fidelity neural audio compression.\n400\t\n401\tTrans. Mach. Learn. Res. 2023.\n402\t\n403\tCited by: §2.2.\n404\t\n405\t[5]\n406\tP. Dhariwal, H. Jun, C. Payne, J. W. Kim, A. Radford, and I. Sutskever (2020)\n407\t\n408\tJukebox: A generative model for music.\n409\t\n410\tCoRR abs/2005.00341.\n411\t\n412\tCited by: §2.2.\n413\t\n414\t[6]\n415\tS. Gulati, J. Serrà, V. Ishwar, and X. Serra (2014)\n416\t\n417\tMining melodic patterns in large audio collections of indian art music.\n418\t\n419\tIn 2014 Tenth International Conference on Signal-Image Technology and Internet-Based Systems,\n420\t\n421\tpp. 264–271.\n422\t\n423\tCited by: §2.1.\n424\t\n425\t[7]\n426\tS. Han, H. Ihm, M. Lee, and W. Lim (2022)\n427\t\n428\tSymbolic music loop generation with neural discrete representations.\n429\t\n430\tIn Proceedings of the 23rd International Society for Music Information\n431\tRetrieval Conference, ISMIR 2022, Bengaluru, India, December 4-8,\n432\t2022,\n433\t\n434\tpp. 403–410.\n435\t\n436\tCited by: §2.2.\n437\t\n438\t[8]\n439\tJ. Jo (2000)\n440\t\n441\tA study on the Shigimsae of Pansori Kyemyunjo.\n442\t\n443\tMaster’s thesis, Hanyang University, Seoul, Republic of Korea.\n444\t\n445\tNote: Original title in Korean: 판소리 계면조 시김새에 관한 고찰 [조주선]\n446\t\n447\tCited by: §7.\n448\t\n449\t[9]\n450\tJ. W. Kim, J. Salamon, P. Li, and J. P. Bello (2018)\n451\t\n452\tCrepe: A convolutional representation for pitch estimation.\n453\t\n454\tIn 2018 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal\n455\tProcessing, ICASSP 2018, Calgary, AB, Canada, April 15-20, 2018,\n456\t\n457\tpp. 161–165.\n458\t\n459\tCited by: §4.1.1.\n460\t\n461\t[10]\n462\tS. Kumar, P. Singh, and V. Arora (2025)\n463\t\n464\tRecognizing ornaments in vocal indian art music with active annotation.\n465\t\n466\tCoRR abs/2505.04419.\n467\t\n468\tCited by: §6.2,\n469\tTable 2.\n470\t\n471\t[11]\n472\tL. McInnes and J. Healy (2018)\n473\t\n474\tUMAP: uniform manifold approximation and projection for dimension reduction.\n475\t\n476\tCoRR abs/1802.03426.\n477\t\n478\tCited by: §4.2.\n479\t\n480\t[12]\n481\tF. C. Moss, M. Neuwirth, D. Harasim, and M. Rohrmeier (2019)\n482\t\n483\tStatistical characteristics of tonal harmony: a corpus study of beethoven’s string quartets.\n484\t\n485\tPLoS One 14 (6), pp. e0217242.\n486\t\n487\tCited by: §1.\n488\t\n489\t[13]\n490\tJ. Myers and D. Neuman (2025)\n491\t\n492\tBeyond notation: a digital platform for transcribing and analyzing oral melodic traditions.\n493\t\n494\tIn Proceedings of the 26th International Society for\n495\tMusic Information Retrieval Conference,\n496\t\n497\tpp. 421–429.\n498\t\n499\tCited by: §1.\n500\t\n501\t[14]\n502\tT. Nuttall, G. Plaja-Roglans, L. Pearson, and X. Serra (2021)\n503\t\n504\tThe matrix profile for motif discovery in audio - an example application in carnatic music.\n505\t\n506\tIn Music in the AI Era - 15th International Symposium, CMMR 2021,\n507\tVirtual Event, November 15-19, 2021, Revised Selected Papers,\n508\t\n509\tpp. 228–237.\n510\t\n511\tCited by: §2.1.\n512\t\n513\t[15]\n514\tT. Nuttall, X. Serra, and L. Pearson (2024)\n515\t\n516\tSvara-forms and coarticulation in carnatic music: an investigation using deep clustering.\n517\t\n518\tIn Proceedings of the 11th International Conference on Digital Libraries\n519\tfor Musicology, DLfM 2024, Stellenbosch, South Africa, 27 June 2024,\n520\t\n521\tpp. 15–22.\n522\t\n523\tCited by: §4.2.\n524\t\n525\t[16]\n526\tT. Nuttall, X. Serra, and L. Pearson (2025)\n527\t\n528\tSvara-forms in carnatic music: contextual influences on the performance of svara.\n529\t\n530\tIn IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech, and Signal Processing,\n531\tICASSP 2025 - Workshops, Hyderabad, India, April 6-11, 2025,\n532\t\n533\tpp. 1–5.\n534\t\n535\tCited by: §1.\n536\t\n537\t[17]\n538\tH. G. Ranjani, D. Paramashivan, and T. V. Sreenivas (2017)\n539\t\n540\tQuantized melodic contours in indian art music perception: application to transcription.\n541\t\n542\tIn Proceedings of the 18th International Society for Music Information\n543\tRetrieval Conference, ISMIR 2017, Suzhou, China, October 23-27,\n544\t2017, S. J. Cunningham, Z. Duan, X. Hu, and D. Turnbull (Eds.),\n545\t\n546\tpp. 174–180.\n547\t\n548\tExternal Links: Link\n549\t\n550\tCited by: §2.1.\n551\t\n552\t[18]\n553\tH. G. Ranjani, A. Srinivasamurthy, D. Paramashivan, and T. V. Sreenivas (2019)\n554\t\n555\tA compact pitch and time representation for melodic contours in indian art music.\n556\t\n557\tThe Journal of the Acoustical Society of America 145 (1), pp. 597–603.\n558\t\n559\tCited by: §2.1.\n560\t\n561\t[19]\n562\tS. Rouard, F. Massa, and A. Défossez (2023)\n563\t\n564\tHybrid transformers for music source separation.\n565\t\n566\tIn IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n567\tICASSP 2023, Rhodes Island, Greece, June 4-10, 2023,\n568\t\n569\tpp. 1–5.\n570\t\n571\tCited by: §4.1.\n572\t\n573\t[20]\n574\tN. N. Shikarpur, K. M. Dendukuri, Y. Wu, A. Caillon, and C. A. Huang (2024)\n575\t\n576\tHierarchical generative modeling of melodic vocal contours in hindustani classical music.\n577\t\n578\tIn Proceedings of the 25th International Society for Music Information\n579\tRetrieval Conference, ISMIR 2024, San Francisco, California, USA\n580\tand Online, November 10-14, 2024,\n581\t\n582\tpp. 1020–1028.\n583\t\n584\tCited by: §2.1.\n585\t\n586\t[21]\n587\tD. Temperley (2014)\n588\t\n589\tInformation flow and repetition in music.\n590\t\n591\tJournal of Music Theory 58 (2), pp. 155–178.\n592\t\n593\tCited by: §1.\n594\t\n595\t[22]\n596\tThe Open AI Dataset Project (AI-Hub, S. Korea) (2022)\n597\t\n598\tKorean traditional music score and audio dataset.\n599\t\n600\tNote: AI-HubDataset No. 71470. Available at\n601\thttps://aihub.or.kr/aihubdata/data/view.do?dataSetSn=71470\n602\t\n603\tCited by: §6.1,\n604\tTable 2.\n605\t\n606\t[23]\n607\tA. van den Oord, O. Vinyals, and K. Kavukcuoglu (2017)\n608\t\n609\tNeural discrete representation learning.\n610\t\n611\tIn Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 30: Annual Conference\n612\ton Neural Information Processing Systems 2017, December 4-9, 2017,\n613\tLong Beach, CA, USA,\n614\t\n615\tpp. 6306–6315.\n616\t\n617\tCited by: §2.2,\n618\t§4.2.\n619\t\n620\t[24]\n621\tG. Widmer, S. Dixon, W. Goebl, E. Pampalk, and A. Tobudic (2003)\n622\t\n623\tIn search of the horowitz factor.\n624\t\n625\tAI Magazine 24 (3), pp. 111–111.\n626\t\n627\tCited by: §1.\n628\t\n629\t[25]\n630\tN. Zeghidour, A. Luebs, A. Omran, J. Skoglund, and M. Tagliasacchi (2022)\n631\t\n632\tSoundStream: an end-to-end neural audio codec.\n633\t\n634\tIEEE ACM Trans. Audio Speech Lang. Process. 30, pp. 495–507.\n635\t\n636\tCited by: §2.2.\n637\t\n638\t Major funding support from\n639\t\n640\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ee312e58-0c88-4e9f-ad8c-2c50c5143559","uuid":"d4ffba89-0e8f-4955-a826-9626c277b62c","timestamp":"2026-08-12T10:47:41.715Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/paper_2608.10979_text.txt","content":"\n\nPitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis\n\n Title:\n\n Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\n\n Description:\n\n arXiv is now an independent nonprofit!\n Learn more\n ×\n\n License: CC BY 4.0\n\narXiv:2608.10979v1 [cs.SD] 11 Aug 2026\n\nPitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis\n\nAbstract\n\nComputational analysis of music often relies on discrete representations, yet many musical traditions are organized around continuous pitch movement that resists segmentation into note-like units. For such traditions, the discrete units that analysis would build on are not given in advance. We address this gap by learning a vocabulary of local pitch-contour patterns directly from unlabeled audio, using a VQ-VAE that quantizes fixed-length contour segments into a finite codebook. To make the learned tokens stable across segmentation positions and small variations in timing and pitch range, we train the model with a reconstruction objective evaluated under the best alignment among a set of candidate temporal and pitch-domain transformations. Applied to Korean traditional music, the learned tokens recover information about expert-defined sigimsae categories without supervision, and in pansori individual tokens align with the two principal modes, Gyemyeonjo and Ujo, supporting their use as units for corpus-level analysis of contour-centric traditions.\n\n1 Introduction\n\nFigure 1: Overview of the proposed model.\n\nRepresenting music as sequences of discrete units has been one of the central abstractions underlying computational musicology and symbolic music information retrieval. Its analytical value lies in the fact that transitions between musical events can be counted and modeled statistically. For example, corpus-based chord progression analysis [12] can construct transition matrices between chord symbols and examine their distributional properties, which is possible because harmonic events are represented as discrete categories. A similar principle underlies probabilistic models of melody: once a melody is represented as a sequence of symbolic events, the probability or information content of each note can be estimated from its musical context. Temperley’s theory of uniform information density in music builds on this idea, explaining musical organization in terms of the probability and information density of musical events [21]. The same logic also extends to performance data: MIDI represents performances as note events with onset time, duration, pitch, and velocity, allowing timing and dynamics to be analyzed as event-level attributes [24]. In this sense, symbolic representations are not merely encodings of music, but interfaces that make musical structure available to computational analysis.\n\nThe utility of discrete representations, however, is fundamentally contingent on the availability of appropriate discrete units. Automatic music transcription (AMT) has made substantial progress in converting audio into note-level symbolic representations, yet existing systems are developed and evaluated primarily on Western music [2]. This limitation is not merely a matter of musical style or timbral diversity; it reflects an assumption that musically meaningful events can be segmented into note-like units with relatively well-defined pitch and duration. Many musical traditions do not fully conform to this premise. In such traditions, expressive content is often realized as continuous pitch movement rather than as a succession of clearly bounded note events, making conventional transcription pipelines ill-suited as a basis for analysis.\n\nOne way to obtain discrete units in such traditions is to rely on human expertise.\nSome studies use expert-labeled events directly: for instance, a recent study on Carnatic music used expert-labeled svara and svara-form annotations to show that the svara-form performed for a given svara is strongly influenced by neighboring svaras [16].\nOthers define a vocabulary of intermediate units in advance and transcribe recordings into it: the IDTAP platform, for example, represents Hindustani melodic practice using manually specified pitch-contour trajectories as a middle-level representation between raw F0 curves and conventional notation [13].\nBoth directions show that discrete representations of contour-centric music can support analysis, but they share a reliance on predefined categories and manual labor, which is costly to scale and ill-suited to traditions where the relevant ornamental vocabulary is not formally codified.\nThis motivates a different question: can we learn a discrete vocabulary of contour units directly from data?\n\nIn this work, we investigate this question through VQ-VAE-based pitch contour tokenization.\nVQ-VAE provides a natural framework for this purpose because it learns a finite set of latent codes while preserving enough information to reconstruct the input contour. We use this mechanism not as a generative objective in itself, but as a way to induce a compact vocabulary of local contour patterns that can be used for musical analysis.\nWe apply this approach to sigimsae, the ornamental system of Korean traditional music, where expressive musical units are often realized as continuous pitch movements rather than conventional note events.\n\nOur contribution is threefold: (i) a VQ-VAE-based pitch-contour tokenization model that learns a discrete vocabulary of local contour patterns without labels; (ii) a transformation-minimized reconstruction loss that makes token learning robust to small temporal and pitch-domain variations; and (iii) an evaluation via segmentation-shift consistency, sigimsae classification, and qualitative mode analysis in pansori. Our code and a demo page with audio examples are publicly available.11\n 1\n\n Codebase: https://github.com/SeongUkJu/pitch-contour-tokenizer, Demo: https://seongukju.github.io/pitch-contour-tokenizer-page/\n\n2 Related Work\n\n2.1 Pitch-Contour Abstractions for Ornamental Music\n\nA notable precedent is found in Indian art music, where quantized pitch contours have served as an intermediate representation capturing ornamental detail beyond note-level transcription.\nRanjani et al. [17, 18] mapped each pitch value onto a music-theoretically defined grid of note positions and showed that quantizing only at contour critical points preserves rāga and gamaka characteristics.\nShikarpur et al. [20] adopted finely quantized pitch contours (10-cent bins) as an intermediate representation for hierarchical generative modeling of Hindustani vocal music.\nBoth discretize pitch values onto a predefined grid; we instead learn a vocabulary over local pitch patterns (fixed-length contour segments) directly from unlabeled data.\n\nA separate line of work mines recurring melodic patterns from raw contours via similarity search.\nGulati et al. [6] used DTW-based similarity over a 365-hour Carnatic corpus, and Nuttall et al. [14] applied the matrix profile to find repeated motifs within a single recording.\nThese methods identify a small set of variable-length, recurrence-based instances that highlight reuse, but they do not yield a reusable vocabulary that covers every frame.\nOur tokenizer, by contrast, aims to produce a dense token sequence over the entire contour using a fixed codebook, which makes the resulting representation directly amenable to corpus-level statistical analysis.\n\n2.2 VQ-VAE and Discrete Representation Learning\n\nVQ-VAE [23] learns discrete latent codes through\nreconstruction-based training. Given an input 𝐱\\mathbf{x}, an encoder\nfθf_{\\theta} produces a continuous embedding 𝐳e=fθ​(𝐱)\\mathbf{z}_{e}=f_{\\theta}(\\mathbf{x}),\nwhich is replaced by its nearest entry in a learned codebook\nℰ={𝐞k}k=1K\\mathcal{E}=\\{\\mathbf{e}_{k}\\}_{k=1}^{K} via\n𝐳q=𝐞k∗\\mathbf{z}_{q}=\\mathbf{e}_{k^{\\ast}}, k∗=arg⁡mink⁡‖𝐳e−𝐞k‖2k^{\\ast}=\\arg\\min_{k}\\|\\mathbf{z}_{e}-\\mathbf{e}_{k}\\|_{2},\nand a decoder gϕg_{\\phi} reconstructs 𝐱^=gϕ​(𝐳q)\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}=g_{\\phi}(\\mathbf{z}_{q}).\nThe model is trained with\n\nℒ=ℒrec+‖sg⁡[𝐳e]−𝐳q‖22+β​‖𝐳e−sg⁡[𝐳q]‖22,\\mathcal{L}=\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{rec}}+\\|\\mathrm{sg}[\\mathbf{z}_{e}]-\\mathbf{z}_{q}\\|_{2}^{2}+\\beta\\,\\|\\mathbf{z}_{e}-\\mathrm{sg}[\\mathbf{z}_{q}]\\|_{2}^{2},\n\n(1)\n\nwhere sg⁡[⋅]\\mathrm{sg}[\\cdot] is the stop-gradient operator and gradients flow\nthrough the quantization step via the straight-through estimator.\n\nIn music and audio, vector quantization has been used primarily for generation and compression of audio waveforms or symbolic sequences [7, 5, 25, 4], where codes are optimized for fidelity or bitrate rather than interpretability.\n\nOur use of VQ-VAE differs from both lines: the input is a one-dimensional\npitch contour 𝐱∈ℝL\\mathbf{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L}, and the learned codes are\nintended as interpretable analytical units rather than for generation or\ncompression.\nIn speech, vector-quantized autoencoders applied to raw or spectral audio have been shown to discover units that align with phonetic content [3], supporting the use of VQ-VAE for unsupervised unit discovery from continuous signals.\n\n3 VQ-VAE on Pitch Contour\n\nThe overall architecture is based on VQ-VAE, using a stack of 1D convolutional layers, with two key design choices to enable interpretable, segmentation-robust (position-invariant) tokenization of pitch contours, as illustrated in Figure 1.\n\n3.1 Receptive Field Separation\n\nTo ensure a 1:1 correspondence between each discrete token and a fixed-length pitch contour segment, the input is pre-segmented into non-overlapping windows of a fixed size before encoding.\nThis prevents inter-token dependency during decoding and promotes interpretability of the learned codebook by constraining each token to represent a single localized contour segment.\n\nTo direct the codebook toward capturing ornamental contour patterns rather than absolute pitch levels, we subtract the median pitch value of each receptive field segment before encoding.\nThis makes the objective insensitive to absolute pitch offset and encourages each entry to represent local movements such as oscillations and slides.\nWhen needed for visualization of the full contour sequence, the decoded contour can be shifted back by adding the subtracted median.\n\n3.2 Transformation-Minimized Reconstruction Loss\n\nBecause the input contour is divided into fixed-size non-overlapping segments before encoding, the assigned token can be sensitive to the arbitrary segmentation position.\nFor example, if a vibrato pattern is shifted by only a few frames, the phase observed within a segment changes, although the underlying oscillatory pattern remains the same.\nThis matters because the model is trained with a frame-level MSE reconstruction loss:\neven when two contours correspond to the same local ornamental pattern, small temporal misalignments can lead to large reconstruction errors.\nWithout an alignment-aware objective, the model may encode incidental frame-level differences as separate entries rather than a stable representation of the underlying pattern.\n\nWe address this issue by minimizing the reconstruction error over four transformation factors: (i) temporal offset, which accounts for arbitrary segmentation position;\n(ii) temporal scale, which accounts for small duration differences;\n(iii) pitch offset, which accounts for residual vertical mismatch between the target and reconstruction; and\n(iv) pitch-range scale, which accounts for amplitude or range differences in otherwise similar contour shapes.\nWe define a set of candidate transformations consisting of temporal alignment, pitch shifting, and pitch-range scaling.\n\nLet 𝐱∈ℝL\\mathbf{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L} be a median-normalized input segment and 𝐱^∈ℝL′\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L^{\\prime}} be the decoder output, where L′>LL^{\\prime}>L.\n\nWe define a transformed reconstruction candidate as\n\nTs,τ,a,b​(𝐱^)=a⋅Cτ​(Rs​(𝐱^))+b,T_{s,\\tau,a,b}(\\hat{\\mathbf{x}})=a\\cdot C_{\\tau}\\left(R_{s}(\\hat{\\mathbf{x}})\\right)+b,\n\n(2)\n\nwhere Rs​(⋅)R_{s}(\\cdot) resamples the decoded contour with temporal scale ss, Cτ​(⋅)C_{\\tau}(\\cdot) extracts a length-LL crop at temporal offset τ\\tau, aa scales the pitch range, and bb shifts the pitch level.\nA length-LL crop is then extracted with temporal offset τ∈𝒯\\tau\\in\\mathcal{T}:\n\n𝐱^(s,τ)=Cτ​(𝐱^(s)),\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}^{(s,\\tau)}=C_{\\tau}\\left(\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}^{(s)}\\right),\n\n(3)\n\nwhere Cτ​(⋅)C_{\\tau}(\\cdot) returns a contiguous segment of length LL starting at offset τ\\tau.\n\nThe reconstruction loss is computed as the minimum MSE over all candidate transformations:\n\nℒrec=mins∈𝒮,τ∈𝒯,a∈𝒜,b∈ℬ⁡1L​‖𝐱−[a⋅Cτ​(Rs​(𝐱^))+b]‖22.\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{rec}}=\\min_{s\\in\\mathcal{S},\\ \\tau\\in\\mathcal{T},\\ a\\in\\mathcal{A},\\ b\\in\\mathcal{B}}\\frac{1}{L}\\left\\|\\mathbf{x}-\\left[a\\cdot C_{\\tau}\\left(R_{s}(\\hat{\\mathbf{x}})\\right)+b\\right]\\right\\|_{2}^{2}.\n\n(4)\n\nOnly the transformation attaining the minimum error is used for backpropagation.\n\nThis can be interpreted as fitting the model under the best alignment between the decoded contour and the target segment, rather than penalizing all frame-level discrepancies directly. This objective encourages the codebook to represent contour patterns that are stable under small temporal and pitch-domain variations.\n\nFor example, vibrato contours with similar periodic structure can be mapped to the same token even when they differ in phase or amplitude, because the loss evaluates reconstruction after the best temporal and pitch-domain alignment.\n\n4 Experimental Setup\n\n4.1 Dataset\n\nIn this study, we apply VQ-VAE to pitch contours of Korean traditional music. Although the proposed method is applicable to any pitch contour-based musical tradition, such as Indian art music or pop vocal singing, we focus on Korean traditional music.\nThis is because continuous pitch movement is not merely an expressive embellishment but a central, defining element of the tradition. Unlike Western staff notation, in which music is conceived as a sequence of discrete note events, Korean traditional music, particularly its ornamental system sigimsae, is realized through fluid, continuous pitch trajectories in which the boundary between one “note” and the next is often ambiguous or undefined. This makes it an especially well-suited testbed for a model designed to discover meaningful structure from pitch contours without presupposing discrete note events.\n\nWe selected an in-house pansori recording dataset consisting of approximately 280 hours of audio recordings.\nPansori is a representative vocal genre of Korean traditional music, sung by a solo singer with barrel drum accompaniment.\nIt is well known for its extensive use of melodic ornamentation, making it a particularly rich source of the ornamental patterns investigated in this study.\nFor pansori recordings, the vocal tracks are first isolated using HT-Demucs  [19] to remove instrumental accompaniment.\n\n4.1.1 Pre-processing\n\nWe extract the fundamental frequency (F0) with the pretrained CREPE [9], which estimates F0 values every 10 ms with a confidence score in [0,1][0,1].\nThe extracted F0 values are converted to MIDI scale and normalized by median.\nFrames below a predefined confidence threshold are filtered out.\nWithin each receptive field segment, the gaps of up to two consecutive filtered frames are filled via linear interpolation.\n\nSegments containing extensive unvoiced regions are discarded because our 1D CNN-based tokenizer assumes a dense continuous contour and cannot directly represent missing pitch values.\nFilling such regions with a constant padding value would introduce artificial patterns into the input, which could be absorbed by the codebook and hinder the interpretability of the learned representations in subsequent analysis.\nAddressing unvoiced regions more explicitly is left for future work.\n\nBefore filtering, the data are split 8:1:1 into train, validation, and test sets.\nDuring training, a random start point is sampled from each filtered segment at every epoch, introducing variability in segmentation position, while the validation and test sets use fixed segments for consistent evaluation.\n\n4.2 Model Specification\n\nThe encoder and decoder each consist of 6 stacked one-dimensional convolutional layers with a receptive field size of 128 frames and a codebook of 256 entries.\nThe model is trained using the VQ-VAE objective [23], in which the standard reconstruction loss is replaced by the transformation-minimized reconstruction loss described in Section 3.2, combined with a vector quantization loss with commitment coefficient β=2.0\\beta=2.0.\nTraining is conducted for 50K updates using the AdamW optimizer with an initial learning rate of 0.001 and a batch size of 1024.\nWe select the checkpoint with the lowest validation reconstruction loss.\n\nThe transformation parameters are set empirically.\nThe decoder produces 2​L2L frames, resampled by Rs​(⋅)R_{s}(\\cdot) to lengths LsL_{s} approximately 𝒮={1, 1.08, 1.2}\\mathcal{S}=\\{1,\\,1.08,\\,1.2\\} times LL (i.e., 128128, 139139, and 154154 frames); all valid offsets 𝒯={0,1,…,Ls−L}\\mathcal{T}=\\{0,1,\\dots,L_{s}-L\\} are considered as crops, where LsL_{s} is the resampled length.\nPitch-range scaling and pitch shifting use 𝒜={0.85,1.0,1.15}\\mathcal{A}=\\{0.85,1.0,1.15\\} and ℬ={−0.1,0.0,0.1}\\mathcal{B}=\\{-0.1,0.0,0.1\\}.\n\nFor the comparison, we also trained an Autoencoder Baseline, adapting Nuttall et al. [15], who applied Uniform Manifold Approximation and Projection (UMAP) [11] and K-Means clustering to supervised GRU embeddings.\nOur unsupervised variant trains an autoencoder on the pansori dataset and tokenizes its latent embeddings via z-score normalization, UMAP, and K-Means.\n\n5 Segmentation Shift Consistency\n\nMethod\nKLD ↓\\downarrow\nAcc. ↑\\uparrow\n\n0–60\n0–124\n0–60\n0–124\n\nAutoencoder\n1.747\n2.085\n0.100\n0.115\n\nVQ-VAE\n0.657\n0.859\n0.462\n0.488\n\n  + temporal align\n0.531\n0.735\n0.519\n0.561\n\n  + all transformations\n0.652\n0.881\n0.456\n0.495\n\nTable 1: Token consistency evaluation results. KLD denotes Token Distribution KLD, and Acc. denotes Token Matching Accuracy. Results are reported for two segmentation-offset ranges: 0–60 and 0–124 frames. “+ temporal align” applies temporal offset and temporal-scale transformations, while “+ all transformations” additionally includes pitch offset and pitch-range scaling.\n\nWe introduced the transformation-minimized reconstruction loss to reduce the sensitivity of token assignment to temporal and pitch-domain variations.\nHere, we isolate one of its intended effects: robustness to segmentation position.\nSpecifically, we test whether the same long pitch contour yields similar token sequences when the starting position of the fixed-size segmentation window is shifted.\n\n5.1 Experimental Dataset Construction\n\nFrom the pansori dataset used for VQ-VAE training, we select contour segments that satisfy the filtering criteria, contain no unvoiced frames, and are at least three times longer than the receptive field. For each segment, we generate multiple token sequences by shifting the segmentation start position in increments of 4 frames.\n\nWe report results for two offset ranges: 0–60 frames and 0–124 frames. The former evaluates shifts smaller than half of the 128-frame receptive field, while the latter covers nearly all possible phases within one receptive field and thus provides a stricter test of segmentation robustness.\n\n5.2 Evaluation Metrics\n\nWe use two complementary metrics. Token Distribution KLD measures whether the overall codebook usage remains stable across shifted versions of the same contour. For each segment, we construct a reference token distribution by aggregating token counts across all shifted versions. We then compute the Kullback–Leibler divergence (KLD) between the token distribution of each shifted version and this reference distribution, and average the result over versions and segments. Lower KLD indicates more stable token usage.\n\nToken Matching Accuracy measures local token-level consistency. We use the offset-0 token sequence as the reference and compare each shifted token sequence against it. For offsets below half of the receptive field, tokens are compared at the same sequence index. For offsets of 64 frames or greater, each shifted token is compared with the succeeding reference token, since the shifted receptive field overlaps more strongly with the next reference window. Higher accuracy indicates more consistent token assignment across shifts.\n\n5.3 Results\n\nAs shown in Table 1, the autoencoder baseline is highly sensitive to segmentation shifts, whereas VQ-VAE substantially improves both distribution-level and local token consistency.\n\nAdding temporal alignment gives the best results on this segmentation-specific evaluation, supporting the intended effect of the transformation-minimized reconstruction loss. The full transformation setting performs worse than temporal alignment alone on this metric.\nThis is expected because the additional pitch-domain transformations are not designed specifically for segmentation robustness; rather, they allow similar contour shapes to be matched after pitch shifting or pitch-range scaling.\nAs a result, similar contours may be represented by different tokens whose decoded outputs can still be adjusted to fit the same target, which can reduce exact token consistency under shifted segmentations.\nTheir benefit is therefore evaluated more directly in the downstream sigimsae classification experiment.\n\n6 Sigimsae Classification\n\nIf the learned tokens are to serve as analytical units, their patterns should align with expert-defined ornament categories. We test this with a minimal probing setup, a per-token MAP classifier — that isolates how much label-discriminative information is carried by individual tokens.\n\n6.1 Sigimsae Labeled Dataset\n\nWe employed the annotated Korean traditional music recordings from the NIA AI-Hub dataset [22].\nThe dataset consists of annotated sigimsae segments from monophonic Korean traditional music recordings.\nBecause it is highly class-imbalanced and contains both vocal and instrumental performances, we report results in two settings: the full setting, with 34.0 hours of audio and 42.3k annotated segments totaling 9.3 hours across six classes, and the vocal-only setting, with 10.0 hours of audio and 13.5k annotated segments totaling 2.4 hours across five classes.\n\nThe data are split at the track level with per-class stratification into train, validation, and test sets (approximately 8:1:1), ensuring that segments from the same track do not appear in multiple splits.\n\n6.2 Token-to-label lookup classifier\n\nFor each annotated sigimsae segment, the center portion is cropped to the receptive field size and encoded into a single token.\nSegments shorter than the receptive field are padded with surrounding context.\n\nWe use a simple frequency-based classifier to test how much label-discriminative information is carried by individual tokens. This is not intended to build a strong supervised classifier. Rather, it serves as a probing task: if the tokens learned without sigimsae labels are musically meaningful, their distribution should contain information that is predictive of expert-defined ornament categories.\n\nFrom the training set, we count how often each token occurs with each sigimsae label and use these counts to estimate P⁡(token∣label)P(\\text{token}\\mid\\text{label}).\nClassification is then performed using maximum a posteriori (MAP) estimation:\n\ny^=arg⁡max⁡[τ⋅log⁡P⁡(y)+log⁡P⁡(token∣y)]\\hat{y}=\\arg\\max\\left[\\tau\\cdot\\log P(y)+\\log P(\\text{token}\\mid y)\\right]\n\n(5)\n\nwhere the temperature τ∈[0,1]\\tau\\in[0,1] is selected on the validation set and applied to the test set.\n\nAs a scale reference, we additionally report a supervised Encoder-Decoder Temporal Convolutional Network (ED-TCN), which was employed for Rāga ornamentation detection [10] trained directly on sigimsae classification with median-normalized F0 contours.\nIt uses full label supervision and is not directly comparable, but indicates the order of magnitude reachable with supervised contour-based classification.\n\n6.3 Results\n\nMethod\nF​1m​a​c​r​oF1_{macro} ↑\\uparrow\nm​A​PmAP ↑\\uparrow\n\nAll\nVocal\nAll\nVocal\n\nAutoencoder\n0.284\n0.324\n0.265\n0.290\n\nVQ-VAE\n0.266\n0.319\n0.248\n0.302\n\n  + temporal align\n0.285\n0.320\n0.272\n0.317\n\n  + all transformations\n0.285\n0.331\n0.278\n0.324\n\nED-TCN [10]\n0.463\n0.541\n0.579\n0.531\n\nTable 2: Sigimsae Classification results on NIA AI-Hub dataset [22]. We report F​1m​a​c​r​oF1_{macro} and m​A​PmAP for both all/vocal subset settings.\n\nAs presented in Table 2, vanilla VQ-VAE alone does not outperform the autoencoder baseline in F​1F1. Without alignment-aware training, similar contours are split across distinct codes, fragmenting per-class distributions. Adding the full transformation-minimized loss reverses this and yields the best probing scores across both settings, with a larger gain than temporal alignment alone.\nThe gap to the supervised ED-TCN remains substantial, but a single token recovers roughly three-fifths of its F​1F1 on the vocal setting without sequence modeling or label supervision during representation learning.\n\nalt.\nFigure 2: Decoded contours of the most representative token per sigimsae category, selected by the highest P⁡(token∣label)P(\\textit{token}\\mid\\textit{label}), for VQ-VAE (left) and Autoencoder baseline (right).\n\nFigure 2 visualizes the per-class prototype token (highest OPENP⁡(token∣label))P(\\text{token}\\mid\\text{label})) for each model. For VQ-VAE, the six classes recruit six distinct tokens, each visually matching the canonical realization of its category: the upward-leap-and-glide of southern-style descending flick (Token 58), the monotonic descent of descending slide (Token 178), and three vibrato classes mapped to tokens with clearly distinct oscillation profiles.\nFor the autoencoder, a single token (Token 196) is selected as the prototype for four of the six classes.\nThe autoencoder’s embeddings entangle phase, pitch level, range, and shape, so K-Means cuts across ornament types.\nThe transformation-minimized loss removes these factors during training, leaving contour shape as the main basis for the codebook.\n\nFigure 3: Decoded contours(black) of representative tokens for Gyemyeonjo (top) and Ujo, selected by the highest P⁡(mode∣token)P(\\text{mode}\\mid\\text{token}). Blue and red lines show the median-subtracted original contours assigned to each token for Gyemyeonjo and Ujo, respectively.\n\n7 Token Analysis in Pansori\n\nAs a case study, we explore whether the learned tokenizer can serve as a tool for data-driven musicological analysis by examining how individual tokens relate to the two principal modes of pansori.\n\nDataset:\nPansori has two representative modes: Gyemyeonjo and Ujo, each characterized by distinct melodic and ornamental patterns.\nWe used a mode-annotated subset [1] of our in-house pansori dataset, which was annotated by an expert. The dataset consists of 26.7 hours of Gyemyeonjo annotations and 8.7 hours of Ujo annotations.\n\nMethod: A pansori mode is largely defined by its scale, but each mode is also known to carry characteristic ornamental patterns. Because our tokens are median-normalized and thus carry no absolute pitch information, any mode-specific token usage must arise from contour shape itself. We therefore count token occurrences within mode-labeled segments and identify the tokens with the highest P⁡(mode∣token)P(\\text{mode}\\mid\\text{token}) for each mode, treating these as candidate ornamental patterns characteristic of each mode.\n\nResult: Figure 3 shows the contour of those representative tokens together with examples of input contours assigned to each token. To provide musical context, one of the co-authors also transcribed a slightly extended window around each example into staff notation.\n\nInspecting these tokens confirms that each is indeed characteristic of its associated mode. Token 87 captures the upper turn gesture, and Token 141 captures a downward gliding contour; both are widely described in pansori studies as defining ornamental features of Gyemyeonjo (Score Excerpts 24 and 3 in  [8]).\nTokens 171, in contrast, corresponds to melodic phrase frequently observed in ujo.\n\nToken 54 is particularly informative. The descending major-second motion to the tonic that it captures is a melodic move shared by both Gyemyeonjo and Ujo, yet this specific token occurs predominantly in Ujo. The distinguishing factor is the wide vibrato applied to the upper note in Ujo before it resolves to the tonic. The tokenizer, trained without any mode supervision, separates the two modes along this ornamental dimension, a distinction that note-level transcription would discard since the underlying pitch sequence is identical.\n\nAlthough limited to a single case, this result indicates that the learned tokens can serve as analytical units linking raw contours to higher-level musicological categories. The same codebook, queried against other label sets such as schools or performers, offers a path toward corpus-level study of ornamental practice without requiring predefined ornament categories or note-level transcription.\n\n8 Conclusion and Future Work\n\nWe proposed a VQ-VAE-based tokenizer that learns a discrete vocabulary of local pitch-contour patterns without ornament labels, together with a transformation-minimized reconstruction loss that makes the learned tokens robust to small temporal and pitch-domain variations.\nExperiments on Korean traditional music show improved segmentation-shift consistency and sigimsae classification over baselines, and qualitative analysis on pansori shows that individual tokens separate Gyemyeonjo and Ujo even when the underlying pitch sequence is shared, a distinction that note-level transcription would discard.\n\nOur approach has one main limitation: our 1D-CNN tokenizer assumes a dense continuous contour and cannot directly model unvoiced regions, so we restricted training and evaluation to segments with few unvoiced frames. Extending the model to handle unvoiced regions explicitly is necessary for tokenizing entire recordings end-to-end.\n\nTwo directions remain open for future work. The most immediate is corpus-level musicological analysis: converting long recordings into token sequences and studying transition statistics, n-gram distributions, or conditional patterns of ornamental units across modes, schools, and performers — analyses that have been routine for symbolic Western music but are not yet available for contour-centric traditions. The same framework is also in principle applicable to other such traditions, including Indian art music and pop vocal singing.\n\n9 Acknowledgement\n\nThis work was supported by the National Research Foundation of Korea (NRF) grant funded by the Korea government (MSIT) (RS-2025-00560548).\n\nReferences\n\n[1]\nAnonymous (2026)\n\nFrame-level pansori mode classification with complementary audio representations.\n\nNote: Submitted to ISMIR 2026\n\nCited by: §7.\n\n[2]\nS. Chang, E. Benetos, H. Kirchhoff, and S. Dixon (2024)\n\nYourmt3+: multi-instrument music transcription with enhanced transformer architectures and cross-dataset stem augmentation.\n\nIn 2024 IEEE 34th International Workshop on Machine Learning for Signal Processing (MLSP),\n\npp. 1–6.\n\nCited by: §1.\n\n[3]\nJ. Chorowski, R. J. Weiss, S. Bengio, and A. van den Oord (2019)\n\nUnsupervised speech representation learning using wavenet autoencoders.\n\nIEEE ACM Trans. Audio Speech Lang. Process. 27 (12), pp. 2041–2053.\n\nCited by: §2.2.\n\n[4]\nA. Défossez, J. Copet, G. Synnaeve, and Y. Adi (2023)\n\nHigh fidelity neural audio compression.\n\nTrans. Mach. Learn. Res. 2023.\n\nCited by: §2.2.\n\n[5]\nP. Dhariwal, H. Jun, C. Payne, J. W. Kim, A. Radford, and I. Sutskever (2020)\n\nJukebox: A generative model for music.\n\nCoRR abs/2005.00341.\n\nCited by: §2.2.\n\n[6]\nS. Gulati, J. Serrà, V. Ishwar, and X. Serra (2014)\n\nMining melodic patterns in large audio collections of indian art music.\n\nIn 2014 Tenth International Conference on Signal-Image Technology and Internet-Based Systems,\n\npp. 264–271.\n\nCited by: §2.1.\n\n[7]\nS. Han, H. Ihm, M. Lee, and W. Lim (2022)\n\nSymbolic music loop generation with neural discrete representations.\n\nIn Proceedings of the 23rd International Society for Music Information\nRetrieval Conference, ISMIR 2022, Bengaluru, India, December 4-8,\n2022,\n\npp. 403–410.\n\nCited by: §2.2.\n\n[8]\nJ. Jo (2000)\n\nA study on the Shigimsae of Pansori Kyemyunjo.\n\nMaster’s thesis, Hanyang University, Seoul, Republic of Korea.\n\nNote: Original title in Korean: 판소리 계면조 시김새에 관한 고찰 [조주선]\n\nCited by: §7.\n\n[9]\nJ. W. Kim, J. Salamon, P. Li, and J. P. Bello (2018)\n\nCrepe: A convolutional representation for pitch estimation.\n\nIn 2018 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal\nProcessing, ICASSP 2018, Calgary, AB, Canada, April 15-20, 2018,\n\npp. 161–165.\n\nCited by: §4.1.1.\n\n[10]\nS. Kumar, P. Singh, and V. Arora (2025)\n\nRecognizing ornaments in vocal indian art music with active annotation.\n\nCoRR abs/2505.04419.\n\nCited by: §6.2,\nTable 2.\n\n[11]\nL. McInnes and J. Healy (2018)\n\nUMAP: uniform manifold approximation and projection for dimension reduction.\n\nCoRR abs/1802.03426.\n\nCited by: §4.2.\n\n[12]\nF. C. Moss, M. Neuwirth, D. Harasim, and M. Rohrmeier (2019)\n\nStatistical characteristics of tonal harmony: a corpus study of beethoven’s string quartets.\n\nPLoS One 14 (6), pp. e0217242.\n\nCited by: §1.\n\n[13]\nJ. Myers and D. Neuman (2025)\n\nBeyond notation: a digital platform for transcribing and analyzing oral melodic traditions.\n\nIn Proceedings of the 26th International Society for\nMusic Information Retrieval Conference,\n\npp. 421–429.\n\nCited by: §1.\n\n[14]\nT. Nuttall, G. Plaja-Roglans, L. Pearson, and X. Serra (2021)\n\nThe matrix profile for motif discovery in audio - an example application in carnatic music.\n\nIn Music in the AI Era - 15th International Symposium, CMMR 2021,\nVirtual Event, November 15-19, 2021, Revised Selected Papers,\n\npp. 228–237.\n\nCited by: §2.1.\n\n[15]\nT. Nuttall, X. Serra, and L. Pearson (2024)\n\nSvara-forms and coarticulation in carnatic music: an investigation using deep clustering.\n\nIn Proceedings of the 11th International Conference on Digital Libraries\nfor Musicology, DLfM 2024, Stellenbosch, South Africa, 27 June 2024,\n\npp. 15–22.\n\nCited by: §4.2.\n\n[16]\nT. Nuttall, X. Serra, and L. Pearson (2025)\n\nSvara-forms in carnatic music: contextual influences on the performance of svara.\n\nIn IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech, and Signal Processing,\nICASSP 2025 - Workshops, Hyderabad, India, April 6-11, 2025,\n\npp. 1–5.\n\nCited by: §1.\n\n[17]\nH. G. Ranjani, D. Paramashivan, and T. V. Sreenivas (2017)\n\nQuantized melodic contours in indian art music perception: application to transcription.\n\nIn Proceedings of the 18th International Society for Music Information\nRetrieval Conference, ISMIR 2017, Suzhou, China, October 23-27,\n2017, S. J. Cunningham, Z. Duan, X. Hu, and D. Turnbull (Eds.),\n\npp. 174–180.\n\nExternal Links: Link\n\nCited by: §2.1.\n\n[18]\nH. G. Ranjani, A. Srinivasamurthy, D. Paramashivan, and T. V. Sreenivas (2019)\n\nA compact pitch and time representation for melodic contours in indian art music.\n\nThe Journal of the Acoustical Society of America 145 (1), pp. 597–603.\n\nCited by: §2.1.\n\n[19]\nS. Rouard, F. Massa, and A. Défossez (2023)\n\nHybrid transformers for music source separation.\n\nIn IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\nICASSP 2023, Rhodes Island, Greece, June 4-10, 2023,\n\npp. 1–5.\n\nCited by: §4.1.\n\n[20]\nN. N. Shikarpur, K. M. Dendukuri, Y. Wu, A. Caillon, and C. A. Huang (2024)\n\nHierarchical generative modeling of melodic vocal contours in hindustani classical music.\n\nIn Proceedings of the 25th International Society for Music Information\nRetrieval Conference, ISMIR 2024, San Francisco, California, USA\nand Online, November 10-14, 2024,\n\npp. 1020–1028.\n\nCited by: §2.1.\n\n[21]\nD. Temperley (2014)\n\nInformation flow and repetition in music.\n\nJournal of Music Theory 58 (2), pp. 155–178.\n\nCited by: §1.\n\n[22]\nThe Open AI Dataset Project (AI-Hub, S. Korea) (2022)\n\nKorean traditional music score and audio dataset.\n\nNote: AI-HubDataset No. 71470. Available at\nhttps://aihub.or.kr/aihubdata/data/view.do?dataSetSn=71470\n\nCited by: §6.1,\nTable 2.\n\n[23]\nA. van den Oord, O. Vinyals, and K. Kavukcuoglu (2017)\n\nNeural discrete representation learning.\n\nIn Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 30: Annual Conference\non Neural Information Processing Systems 2017, December 4-9, 2017,\nLong Beach, CA, USA,\n\npp. 6306–6315.\n\nCited by: §2.2,\n§4.2.\n\n[24]\nG. Widmer, S. Dixon, W. Goebl, E. Pampalk, and A. Tobudic (2003)\n\nIn search of the horowitz factor.\n\nAI Magazine 24 (3), pp. 111–111.\n\nCited by: §1.\n\n[25]\nN. Zeghidour, A. Luebs, A. Omran, J. Skoglund, and M. Tagliasacchi (2022)\n\nSoundStream: an end-to-end neural audio codec.\n\nIEEE ACM Trans. Audio Speech Lang. Process. 30, pp. 495–507.\n\nCited by: §2.2.\n\n Major funding support from\n\n","numLines":640,"startLine":1,"totalLines":640}}}
